  "Number": 27,
  "Question": "If \\(f(x)=3x+2\\) for all real \\(x,\\) then the statement:\n\n“\\(|f(x)+4|\\lt a\\) whenever \\(|x+2|\\lt b\\) and \\(a\\gt0\\) and \\(b\\gt0\\)”\n\nis true when",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(b\\le \\frac{a}{3}\\)",
    "B": "\\(b\\gt \\frac{a}{3}\\)",
    "C": "\\(a\\le \\frac{b}{3}\\)",
    "D": "\\(a\\gt \\frac{b}{3}\\)",
    "E": "The statement is never true."
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "A",
  "Solution": "The expressions inside the two absolute values are directly related:\n\\[\n|f(x)+4|=|3x+6|=3|x+2|.\n\\]\nThus we need every distance \\(|x+2|<b\\) to become less than \\(a\\) after multiplication by \\(3\\).\n\nIf \\(b\\le a/3\\), then\n\\[\n|f(x)+4|=3|x+2|<3b\\le a,\n\\]\nso the statement holds. This also explains why \\(b=a/3\\) is allowed: the first inequality remains strict.\n\nConversely, suppose \\(b>a/3\\). Choose \\(x=-2+a/3\\). Since \\(a>0\\), this gives\n\\[\n|x+2|=\\frac a3<b,\n\\qquad\n|f(x)+4|=a,\n\\]\nwhich violates the required strict inequality. Therefore the exact condition is \\(b\\le a/3\\), and the answer is \\(\\boxed{\\text{A}}\\).",
